title: make-live-reorder-backstop Procedure
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

<<.from-version "5.5.0">> The <<.def make-live-reorder-backstop>> [[procedure|Procedures]] renders a drop target covering the whole of a list that is being reordered by dragging, for as long as one of its items is being dragged.

It answers only where the items themselves do not, catching what falls between them: the padding around the list and the ground beyond its ends. As well as letting a drop there land rather than be refused, this is what marks out where the list is, so that a drag crossing such ground is not taken for a drag that has left the list altogether.

It is also how a list that has been emptied is dragged into again. The places an item can be put are carried by the items themselves, so a list with none of them left has nowhere to aim at; this takes the first item back. It does so only while the list is empty, since a list that still has items already offers a place against every one of them, and the ground this covers is as much beside them as below them.

<<.tip """This is used internally by the <<.dlink make-live-reorderable-list "make-live-reorderable-list Procedure">> procedure, which renders a whole list and is what you would normally use. Use this one only to build something that procedure cannot.""">>

It takes <<.param list>>, <<.param enable>>, <<.param group>>, <<.param dragState>>, <<.param dropActions>> and <<.param commit>>, which mean the same as they do for <<.dlink make-live-reorderable-list "make-live-reorderable-list Procedure">>.

There is one occasion to render it yourself. Where several lists are given the same <<.param group>> so that items can be dragged between them, the space around and between those lists belongs to none of them, and a drag crossing it would be taken for a drag that has left them all. Rendering this over the whole arrangement, with only the <<.param group>> given, marks that space as still being part of it.

It must be rendered inside the element the list is laid out in, which must be positioned, and before the items so that they are drawn on top of it. See DragAndDropMechanism for an overview.
